============================================================
LISKOV SUBSTITUTION PRINCIPLE (LSP) – COMPLETE THEORY POINTS
============================================================

🧩 LSP – Liskov Substitution Principle

🔹 One-line Definition
---------------------
A child class should be usable wherever its parent class is used
— without breaking behavior.


🔹 Layman (Very Simple) Definition ⭐
-----------------------------------
“If something claims to be a type of something else,
it should behave exactly like that thing.”

Or even simpler:
👉 If I replace the parent with the child,
nothing should go wrong/non-meaningfull/


🤔 Why This Principle Exists
---------------------------
If a subclass changes the meaning of what the parent promises,
your application breaks in unexpected places.

LSP exists to:
• Protect correctness
• Avoid surprises at runtime
• Ensure inheritance is used safely


============================================================
1️⃣ Formal Definition
============================================================
Liskov Substitution Principle states:
“A subclass must be substitutable for its parent class
without altering the correctness of the program.”


============================================================
2️⃣ Simple Meaning
============================================================
If a parent class object is expected,
you should be able to pass its child class object
WITHOUT causing bugs, crashes, or unexpected behavior.


============================================================
3️⃣ Golden Rule (Easy to Remember) ⭐
============================================================
If replacing Parent with Child causes bugs,
then LSP is BROKEN.


============================================================
4️⃣ Why LSP Is Important
============================================================
• Ensures inheritance is meaningful
• Prevents runtime failures
• Keeps behavior predictable
• Allows safe polymorphism
• Makes systems extensible without fear


============================================================
5️⃣ Common LSP Violations
============================================================
• Throwing exceptions in overridden methods
• Leaving overridden methods empty
• Changing the meaning of a method
• Returning incompatible or unexpected results
• Adding restrictions that parent class never had
• Saying “Not supported” in overridden methods


============================================================
6️⃣ Real-World Analogy (Layman-Friendly) ⭐
============================================================
Statement:
“A bottle is something you can drink from”

Valid substitutions:
• Water bottle ✅
• Juice bottle ✅

Invalid substitution:
• Petrol bottle ❌

Why?
Because petrol cannot be drunk.
Replacing a normal bottle with a petrol bottle causes harm.

➡️ Hence, PetrolBottle should NOT be treated as a Bottle.


============================================================
7️⃣ Another Layman Example
============================================================
Statement:
“A vehicle can be driven”

Valid:
• Car ✅
• Bike ✅

Invalid:
• Toy car ❌

A toy car looks like a vehicle,
but cannot be driven.
Replacing Vehicle with ToyCar breaks behavior.


============================================================
8️⃣ How to Fix LSP Violations
============================================================
• Do NOT use inheritance if behavior differs
• Split behavior using interfaces
• Prefer composition over inheritance
• Model real-world rules correctly
• Ask: “Can I safely replace parent with this child?”


============================================================
9️⃣ LSP in Android Development
============================================================
Common real-world Android cases:
• Custom View extending View must still behave like a View
• RecyclerView.Adapter subclasses must respect adapter contracts
• MediaPlayer subclasses must support play()
• ViewModel subclasses must follow lifecycle expectations
• Fragment subclasses must respect Fragment lifecycle rules


============================================================
🔟 Relation to SOLID
============================================================
LSP is the "L" in SOLID:

• S – Single Responsibility Principle
• O – Open Closed Principle
• L – Liskov Substitution Principle
• I – Interface Segregation Principle
• D – Dependency Inversion Principle


============================================================
🎯 Interview One-Liners (Use Any)
============================================================

• “LSP ensures that child classes can replace parent classes
   without breaking application behavior.”

• “If a subclass breaks the promise of its parent,
   Liskov Substitution Principle is violated.”

• “Inheritance should strengthen behavior, not weaken it.”

